        "math_academy_id": "q-149634",
        "problem": "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},\u2026,{x}_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[2,7]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},\u2026,{y}_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[-3,-2]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}4({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})\u0394{x}_{i}\u0394{y}_{j},$ where $\u0394{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $\u0394{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$",
        "worked_solution": "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})\u0394{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[2,7].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}=7-2 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $j,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})\u0394{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})\u0394{y}_{1}+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})\u0394{y}_{2}+\u22ef+({y}_{n}+{x}_{n-1})\u0394{y}_{n} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}\\,+\\,{y}_{0})({y}_{1}\\,-\\,{y}_{0})\\,+\\,({y}_{2}\\,+\\,{y}_{1})({y}_{2}\\,-\\,{y}_{1})\\,+\\,\u22ef\\,+\\,({y}_{n}\\,+\\,{y}_{n-1})({y}_{n}\\,-\\,{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =({y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2})+({y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2})+\u22ef+({y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{n-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}}-{y}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}})+\u22ef+({y}_{n}^{2}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}^{2}+{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}\u0394{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})\u0394{y}_{j}=4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-5)=-100.\n$$",
